The bug is confirmed. Now let me understand the root cause in the `mri` method of `CAC.ts`.

Looking at the code:

1. First, defaults are set for all option names:
```javascript
for (const cliOption of cliOptions) {
  if (!ignoreDefault && cliOption.config.default !== undefined) {
    for (const name of cliOption.names) {
      options[name] = cliOption.config.default
    }
  }
}
```

2. Then, parsed values are applied:
```javascript
for (const key of Object.keys(parsed)) {
  if (key !== '_') {
    const keys = key.split('.')
    setDotProp(options, keys, parsed[key])
  }
}
```

The issue is that when we parse `--base-url https://gitlab.com`, the mri library only sets `baseUrl` in the parsed object (after camelCase conversion). It doesn't set `b`. So when we apply the defaults first and then the parsed values, `b` keeps the default value.

The fix should be: when setting defaults, check if ANY of the aliases were already parsed. If any alias was parsed, don't set defaults for any of the names.

Let me look at what `parsed` looks like after mri runs. I'll need to understand the mapping better.

Actually, looking at the code more carefully:

```javascript
let parsed = mri(argv, mriOptions)
parsed = Object.keys(parsed).reduce(
  (res, name) => {
    return {
      ...res,
      [camelcaseOptionName(name)]: parsed[name],
    }
  },
  { _: [] }
)
```

So `parsed` contains the camelCased versions of the options. For `--base-url`, it becomes `baseUrl`.

The fix should be: before setting defaults, check if any of the option's names (after camelCase conversion) exist in the parsed object.


The solution is to modify the defaults-setting logic to first check if any alias was already provided by the user. For each option, I need to examine all its names and skip setting defaults if any of those names appear in the parsed object. This prevents the default from overwriting user-provided values on shorter aliases.

